    "evidence_text": "DBLP:conf/nips/KirosZSZUTF15,DBLP:conf/emnlp/ConneauKSBB17,DBLP:conf/iclr/LogeswaranL18,DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1903-09424.\n\nThe prediction of surrounding sentences endows the model with the ability to model the sentence-level coherence.\n\nSkip-Thought  consists of an encoder and two decoders.\n\nWhen a sentence is given and encoded into a vector by the encoder, the decoders are trained to predict the next sentence and the previous sentence.\n\nInferSent  instead pre-trains the model in a manner of supervised learning.\n\nIt uses a large-scale NLI dataset as the\n\npre-training task to learn the sentence representation.\n\nMethod\tOur method follows the same input format and the model architecture with original BERT.\n\nThe proposed method solely concerns the NSP task.\n\nAs reported , the final pre-trained model is able to achieve 97%-98% accuracy on the NSP task.\n\nsuggest the order discrimination is essential for BERT pre-training.\n\nIn order to tell the difference between NSP and PSP, the model has to extract the detailed semantic for inference.\n\nMethod ::: Gathering More Document-level Information\tBeyond NSP and PSP, which enable the model to learn the short-term dependency between sentences, we also propose to expand the scope of discrimination task to further incorporate the document-level information.\n\nNote that these in-adjacent sentences will introduce much more training noise to the model.\n\nIn summary, when A is given, the pre-training example for each label is constructed as follows: IsNext: Choosing the adjacent following sentence as B. IsPrev: Choosing the adjacent previous sentence as B. IsNextInadj: Choosing the in-adjacent following sentence as B. There is a sentence between A and B. IsPrevInadj: Choosing the in-adjacent previous sentence as B. There is a sentence between A and B. DiffDoc: Drawing B randomly from a different document.\n\nThe method is evaluated on the BERTbase model, which has 12 layers, 12 self-attention heads with a hidden size of 768.\n\nTo accelerate the training speed, two-phase training  is adopted.\n\nThe numbers of training steps of two phases are 50K and 40K for the BERTBase model.\n\n0.9, a $\\beta _2$ of 0.999 and a L2 weight decay rate of $0.01$.\n\nThe data used for pre-training is the same as BERT, i.e., English Wikipedia (2500M words) and BookCorpus (800M words) .\n\nWe explore three method settings for comparison.\n\nThe label distribution is 1:1:1.\n\nThe label distribution is 1:1:1:1:1.\n\nAt last, the proposed method is evaluated on several Chinese datasets.\n\nOrder-invariant with PSP\tNSP in the pre-training is useful for NLI and MRC task .\n\nHowever, we suggested that BERT trained with NSP is order-sensitive, i.e., the performance of BERT depends on the order of the input sentence pair.\n\nTo verify our assumption, a primary experiment was conducted.\n\nwas conducted for 3 epochs for all the tasks, with a learning rate of 2e-5.\n\nThe predictions were obtained by evaluating the training checkpoint with the best validation performance.\n\nBesides NLI, our model also performs better than BERTBase in the STS task.\n\nThe improvements suggest that the PSP task encourages the model to learn more detailed semantics in the pre-training, which improves the model on the downstream learning tasks.\n\nThe improvement should be attributed to better semantic representation.\n\n0.3% increase in EM can be obtained.\n\nTABREF26.\n\nFor SQuAD v2.0, our method also achieved about 4% absolute improvement in both EM and F1 against BERTBase.\n\nThe model computes for a concatenation a scalar as the score, which is then used in a softmax layer for the final prediction.\n\nThe data follows the format of SQuAD.\n\nFor Chinese NLP tasks, we pre-train the model using Chinese corpus.\n\nThe experimental results are given in Table TABREF29.\n\nThe proposed method is compared with four models, i.e., BERTBase , BERTBase with whole word masking , ERNIE  and ERNIE 2.0 .\n\nOur method achieves comparable or even better results against ERNIE 2.0 .\n\nNote that the Chinese ERNIE 2.0 is equipped with 5 different\n\nNSP task to provide more document-level information in the pre-training.\n\nDespite the simplicity, extensive experiments demonstrate that the model obtains a better ability in pair-wise semantic reasoning.\n\nOur work suggests that the document-level objective is effective, at least for the BERTbase model.\n\nIn the future, we will investigate the way to take advantages of both large-scale training and our method.",
